The voices named Frank and Francisco
-----------------------------------

`src/engine/voices.c` defines a deep male voice called Frank, and its
description comes from MindMaker's TextAssist build of this engine.  Those files were
supplied to read and are not redistributed here -- `.gitignore` keeps them out.
`lang/spa/engine/voices.c` defines `Francisco`, which is the same three carried-over
parameters on the 1995 Spanish engine, based on its Pedro as Frank is based on
Peter; everything in this section applies to him equally.  The name is this
project's, chosen so that the voice carries a Spanish name among Spanish ones.

TextAssist put a voice layer of its own over the engine and shipped each voice
as a plain-text file of Klatt-style parameters.  `Frank.tav` is twenty-three of
them, and against Bill -- that product's name for voice 0, which is Centigram's
Peter -- eleven differ.  Three of the eleven are carried over, because they are
the three this engine has somewhere to put: `F0Def` 72 rather than 100,
`HeadSize` 1.1 rather than 1.0, and `IntonLevel` 0.7 rather than 1.0.  The
other eight are two shelf filters, a source richness, a noise rate and a sung
pitch rate, all of which shape the spectrum in that product's own synthesiser,
and none of them is reproduced.

`Frank.tav` also carries a 225-point array of glottal pulse samples that
`Bill.tav` does not.  That is data out of the product rather than a description
of it, so it is **not** reproduced here and no part of it appears in this
repository; this engine keeps its own single pulse table.

The product's help screen names its six voice sliders, which identifies two more
of the eleven: `Breath` is Breathiness and `Larynx` is Creakiness.  Frank reads
Breathiness 10 and Creakiness 0, so he is given breathiness -- as aspiration
held under the voice, which is this project's own mechanism, not the product's
-- and no creak at all.  Nothing else of the product
is used, and there was nothing else to take: its engine DLL carries Centigram's
ten-voice table byte-identical to CGRM_EN.DLL's, so the eight voices it
advertises are built above the engine rather than inside it.

What its version resources say, rather than what is remembered about it:

    TAEngine.dll, TTSVoice.dll    Copyright Mindmaker, Inc. 1999
    Clapi.dll                     L&H Copyright, "L&H English clapi"

So the engine in that product is an L&H build of the same synthesiser, as
SPMtv160.dll is -- see docs/SPANISH.md -- and the suite around it is
MindMaker's.

If you hold rights in that product and would rather the voices were not
described here, please open an issue on the repository and they will be removed.

Japanese: naist-jdic and Open JTalk
-----------------------------------

`lang/jpn/data/jadic.bin` is compiled from **naist-jdic**, the morphological
dictionary distributed with Open JTalk, release 1.11 of December 2018.
`lang/jpn/data/jadic.provenance.txt` records the source folder, the release and a
SHA-256 for each input file. It carries, for each of 486,757
entries, the surface, the reading as said and the reading as written, the
accent type, the mora count and the accent chain rule, plus the 1377x1377
connection cost matrix the analyser needs. Without it the Japanese front end
cannot read kanji and has no accent to speak with.

