Neither project's synthesiser is used or referenced. Open JTalk synthesises
with an HMM; OpenTV is a formant synthesiser and the two have nothing in
common below the text analysis. `open_jtalk-1.11/` is a reference checkout
and is not committed -- `tools/gen_ja_dict.py` and `tools/gen_ja_rules.py`
are how `lang/jpn/data/jadic.bin` and the rule tables were produced from it, and
they are kept in the tree so that anyone with the checkout can confirm that
what is committed here is what comes out of it.

The original binaries
---------------------

No Centigram binary is in this repository, and none is needed to build or
run OpenTV.

A copy is needed for one thing only: `tools/difftest.py`, which runs the
corpus through the original DLL and through OpenTV and requires the audio to
be identical. That comparison is the evidence the decompilation is correct.
Without a copy the test reports that it has nothing to compare against and
everything else still works.

`tools/extract_data.py` is how `data/` was produced, and is kept in the tree
so that anyone with the original can check that what is committed here is
what comes out of it.

Other systems read as references
--------------------------------

Three shipping Japanese text-to-speech systems were inspected locally, from
licensed installations, to answer one design question: whether a Japanese
system reads an English word by looking it up or by rule. They are Microsoft's
OneCore Japanese voices, Lernout & Hauspie's TTS3000, and IBM/Eloquence
ViaVoice. `lang/jpn/research/open_questions.md` section 4.3 records what was observed --
file sizes, the shape of the data, and a module's exported function names.

**Nothing in this repository is derived from any of them.** No code was
disassembled, nothing was executed, and no rule table, lexicon or other data
was taken. What was learned is architectural: that all three pronounce
words beyond a fixed list, and that L&H exposed one G2P interface per language
with a user-dictionary hook. How any of them divides the work between rules
and lexical data is not something an outside inspection settles, and this
makes no claim about it. Those are observations about a design, and the design
is not what is being reproduced here.
